# === VoxelEngine monorepo .gitignore ===

# Node
**/node_modules/
**/package-lock.json
npm-debug.log*

# Python
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.venv/
# per-tool venvs (created on the rig by the install buttons, not committed)
camoufox_env/
scrapling_env/
doc_env/

# Bridge runtime state + caches (regenerated; some hold local data)
**/asset_library/
**/tts-out/
**/doc-out/
**/petfbi-board.json
**/petfbi-counts.json
**/gen-queue.json
**/toasts.json
**/uitars.json

# Secrets / local config — these live in ~/.voxelbridge, but never commit copies
**/.voxelbridge/
.env
*.env
**/trader.json
**/petfbi.json
**/tts.json
# v4063 -- HOME-INFRASTRUCTURE BEARER CREDENTIALS, and one of these two lines closes a gap that was
# already open rather than one this round created. envoy.config.json holds the Enphase JWT that
# ai-bridge/envoySolar.js uses to read the solar array and battery straight off the IQ Gateway --
# a credential for Keith's house, at ~1 year of validity. ha.config.json has held the Home Assistant
# long-lived token since v740 and was NEVER LISTED HERE: it is absent from every commit only because
# no box that ran `git add -A` happened to have one on disk, which is luck rather than a rule.
# Both files are written 0600 by their modules; this is the second half of that.
**/envoy.config.json
**/ha.config.json
*app*password*
*apppw*

# Build artifacts + working forks
EngineProject_*/
*.zip
*.tar.gz
# ...but voxelcaster.zip is a DELIVERABLE, not a build artifact: it is the sideloaded
# Roku channel (manifest + icon + 78 lines of BrightScript, 1.7 KB) that ai-bridge/
# server.js POSTs to a TV's /plugin_install, and that ui/rokuRemotePanel.js offers as
# "via sideloaded VoxelCaster channel". The *.zip rule above was aimed at EngineProject_*
# forks and caught this as collateral; without it a fresh clone's cast button fails with
# "couldn't read channel zip". The tools/ship/deleted/deleted_*.zip tombstones stay out.
!WebGLEngine/roku-channel/voxelcaster.zip

# Large/generated binaries — uncomment if you'd rather keep them off-repo (use Git LFS)
# **/*.onnx
# **/*.vox
# (the small sample GLBs in GPU_Assets are ~200KB each and fine to commit)

# OS / editor junk
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# === Held back from the public mirror (v3845) ===
# Keith's call: the CODE ships, the internal working record does not. These are the
# session notes, not build inputs. They ride in the versioned zip, which remains the
# complete build.
#
# okf/claims/ WAS held back and is NOT any more: it is read by main.js,
# predictions.html, ai-bridge/okfService-selfcheck.mjs and tools/ship/orphanTriage*,
# so withholding it left the mirror unable to run its own gates. Project data, not a
# personal note -- scanned for credentials and personal data before publishing, clean.
# okf/log.md stays out and costs nothing: the one "log.md" reference in
# predictions.html is PROSE inside a why: string describing the OKF spec, not a read.
BACKLOG.md
TODO.md
# v3940 -- changelog.mjs leaves a .bak beside each file it prepends to (that backup is what makes the
# write survivable). BACKLOG.md and TODO.md are held back from the public mirror deliberately, so their
# BACKUPS ARE THE SAME SESSION NOTES UNDER A DIFFERENT NAME -- and nothing was stopping a `git add -A`
# from committing the copy while the original stayed ignored.
BACKLOG.md.bak
TODO.md.bak
WebGLEngine/okf/log.md
Root Utils/email-draft.md

# v4365 -- a generated img2threejs model the rig may drop here for tools/ship/img2three-selfcheck.mjs section 3.
# The img2threejs TOOLING repo is Apache-2.0; the SHOWCASE repo that holds the generated models carries NO LICENSE
# file and no license field in its package.json, so nothing from it is vendored or mirrored. The gate passes with
# the file absent and says the numbers are unsigned; present, it re-derives them.
WebGLEngine/.img2threejs/

# v3923 -- per-host observed gate runtimes. gate-timings.json is the shipped REFERENCE; this is one
# box's comparison against it and must never travel, or a fast machine would export its scale to a slow one.
WebGLEngine/tools/ship/host-timings.local.json
# v4159 -- the pointer to the VBA archive on THIS machine. The archive itself is not in this repository
# (the transmitter, the OpenGL render engine and the connector workbook ship separately); this file only says
# where a copy of it lives here. Deleting it unlinks the archive and changes nothing else.
WebGLEngine/tools/ship/vba-archive.local.json
# v3951 -- install-history.json is the SAME KIND OF THING one line up: ai-bridge/installHistory.js writes THIS
# box's boot timings into it (installToReadyMs, bootAt, downMs), so it is a per-machine measurement and not
# source. It appeared untracked during a routine round; committing it would have exported a sandbox's boot times
# into the rig's tree, which is the export-your-scale hazard the line above exists to prevent.
WebGLEngine/tools/ship/install-history.json

# v3936 -- SHIP-RUN DIAGNOSTICS, DELIBERATELY LOCAL.
# refusal() writes a step's full output here when the ritual stops, because a 2h24m sweep that reports
# "29 FAILED, 11 TIMED OUT" and prints eight names is unrepeatable otherwise. That log is for whoever ran the
# ritual on THAT box: it is one machine's run, it is rewritten by the next one, and committing it would churn
# on every ship while telling a reader of the repo nothing they can act on.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE tools/ship/deleted/ CASE, and the difference is worth stating because the two look alike.
# A deletion receipt is a claim about the tree that must outlive the machine, which is exactly why v3936 moved
# that record to a TRACKED file after finding the zips could never survive a clone. A refusal log is a claim
# about one run. Durable record: tracked. Run artifact: ignored.
WebGLEngine/tools/ship/last-refusal-*.log
WebGLEngine/tools/ship/last-selfchecks-full.log

# v4408 -- A LOCKFILE THIS TREE HAS NEVER TRACKED, WRITTEN BY A GATE.
# ai-bridge/bun.lock appeared untracked mid-ritual: a selfcheck runs `bun install` and bun writes the lockfile
# as a side effect, which is the same class as the sweep rewriting sweep-timings.json -- a gate finishing
# mid-ship dirties a tree that was already staged. NO lockfile is tracked anywhere in this repository (checked:
# `git ls-files | grep bun.lock` and package-lock, both empty), so committing this one would make a policy
# change nobody asked for, on a file whose contents depend on when the box last resolved a registry.
#
# The v3936 rule above decides it: DURABLE RECORD, TRACKED. RUN ARTIFACT, IGNORED. A lockfile is a claim about
# one machine's resolution of a dependency set at one moment. If this tree ever wants reproducible installs,
# that is a decision to make on purpose for every package.json at once -- not one file at a time, and not as
# the accidental residue of a gate run.
WebGLEngine/ai-bridge/bun.lock
